<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berlin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Berlin-Cölln</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berlin is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It grew from medieval trading towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> into the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> and, from 1871, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. It hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> of 1878 and the Berlin Conference on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> of 1884–1885. Divided after 1945 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> from 1961 to 1989, it became the capital of reunited Germany in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin.jpg" alt="Berlin" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kasa Fue; derivative work by Georgfotoart · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museumsinsel_Berlin_Juli_2021_1_(cropped)_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Berlin lies in low-lying marshy woodland on the Northern European Plain, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, which empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> in the western borough of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spandau">Spandau</a>. The Spree follows the Berliner Urstromtal, an ice-age glacial valley between the Barnim Plateau to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teltow">Teltow</a> plateau to the south, formed by meltwater at the end of the last glaciation. About a third of the city's area is forests, parks, gardens, rivers, canals and lakes. The name has roots in the language of the West Slavs and may be related to the Old Polabian stem berl-/birl-, 'swamp'. Five of the twelve boroughs bear Slavic-derived names, among them Pankow and Spandau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled for millennia: a deer mask of about 9,000 BCE is attributed to the Maglemosian culture, and dense settlement along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> gave rise to the Lusatian culture around 2,000 BCE. Germanic tribes settled from about 500 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a> after about 200 CE, and in the 7th century Slavic tribes, later known as the Hevelli and Sprevane, reached the region.</p><p>Berlin was first documented in the 13th century, at the crossing of two important trade routes. It was capital of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from 1417, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> from 1701 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> from 1871. In 1685 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam">Edict of Potsdam</a> granted religious freedom and tax-free status to French Huguenot refugees.</p><p>The Greater Berlin Act of 1920 raised the population from 1.9 million to 4 million, and in the 1920s Berlin was the third-largest city in the world. It was capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and of Nazi Germany until 1945. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was split into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> and East Berlin, divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> became the West German capital. Berlin again became the capital of all Germany after reunification on 3 October 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Berlin covers 891 km2 and is the most populous city proper in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Since 1990 it has been one of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> among the 16 states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, governed by the Senate and the 141-seat Abgeordnetenhaus. The bombing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> devastated the city, and many surviving buildings were later demolished; after reunification many heritage structures were reconstructed.</p><p>Museum Island, designated a district dedicated to art and antiquities by royal decree in 1841, is one of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The Charité hospital traces its origins to 1710, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-koch">Robert Koch</a> and Rudolf Virchow worked in Berlin. The city hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1936-summer-olympics">1936 Summer Olympics</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berlin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Free trade, promised at <b>Berlin</b>, effectively vanished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Junkers opposed Hohenzollern rule from <b>Berlin</b>, and most of society kept the tie with Poland as a safeguard against absolutism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the surrender of 8 May 1945 the Allies abolished the German state in law and divided <b>Berlin</b> and the remaining territory into four occupation zones; around 12 million ethnic Germans had been expelled from across Eastern Europe, and Germany had lost roughly a quarter of its pre-war…</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The German revolution, begun by the sailors' mutiny at Kiel, reached <b>Berlin</b> on 9 November 1918, when Max von Baden announced the abdication of Wilhelm II as Emperor and King of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia, represented at the restored Diet by Otto von Bismarck from 1851, then contested Austria’s leadership at every turn, while the Zollverein bound the smaller states’ economies to <b>Berlin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>About 70,000 Africans worked on the plantations; local officials often colluded with landowners in forcing them to work, although <b>Berlin</b> had banned forced labour, and labour migration devastated 'labour reservoirs' such as Unyamwezi and Usukuma.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
